            <ArticleTitle>Frequency and laparoscopic management of ovarian remnant syndrome.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>33-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To report the frequency and outcome of laparoscopy in women with chronic pelvic pain and/or pelvic mass who were found to have ovarian remnants.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Cohort study. (Canadian Task Force classification II-2).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">University-affiliated hospital.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">One hundred nineteen women who had had hysterectomy and oophorectomy.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTION" NlmCategory="METHODS">Laparoscopic surgery.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MEASUREMENTS AND MAIN RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Ovarian remnants were known in 5 and were found intraoperatively in 21 patients (18%). These 26 women had undergone at least one laparoscopy in an attempt to remove the remnants. After the ureter was identified, ovarian remnants were dissected and removed from the retroperitoneum laparoscopically with minimal risk of vessel or visceral injury. There were no intraoperative or postoperative complications and no conversions to laparotomy. In addition to ovarian remnants, adhesions were found in 19 women, endometriosis in 4, and no other pathology in 3. Twenty women had complete relief of symptoms. At follow-up of 1 to 8 years (mean 5 yrs), six underwent repeat laparoscopy for persistent pain; one had recurrent ovarian remnant.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Ovarian remnant syndrome is not an infrequent complication after hysterectomy and oophorectomy in women with endometriosis.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
